nt from being a very fringe  idea that you  really only find among a handful of  academics or a handful of Fringe  activists mostly on the left although  not entirely - something that now gets  kind of tossed around by CEOs and  political candidates and so on even  something like basic income isn't  necessarily close to being a policy  reality it's certainly something that's  taken seriously as an idea in a way that  it wasn't until fairly recently and one  of the people that's doing that sort of  prominently thanks to some some notable  media appearances over the last three  and six months is 2020 Democratic  presidential candidate Andrew yang and I  I've had some disagreements with him  about the specifics of how he would  implement his what he calls the freedom  dividend $1,000 a month in that I've  pointed out that if you get a thousand  dollars of value from a variety of  welfare programs replacing that with  with a thousand dollars in cash is not  super useful in terms of raising your  standard of living now Andrew argues  that by distinct missing and not means  testing those thousand dollars the  recipient will be better off for example  but that seems sort of marginal to me so  I'm curious if you have any particular  thoughts about his plan and about  implementations that might be better in  your mind  sure so as these things go from what I  understand at least of the contours of  his plan as he's talked about it it's  sort of not the best and not the worst  from my point of view of a basic income  plan now as you sort of alluded to one  of the big questions that arise when  you're evaluating basic income as a  policy idea and as a kind of point of  political principle is what does it both  what does it replace and how much is it  basically the what does it replace  question is very important because as  you said all of these ideas generally I  conceptualize the basic income as  replacing some sort of transfers that  already exist that's part of how it's  funded that can mean just things like  food stamps for example that are  consumed times called near cash benefits  it can include things like means-tested  welfare payments you know gang I think  also talks about disability payments  being replaced which then raises  some questions that maybe we can get  into which is a little dicey er I do  think though that when you're talking  about replacing cash transfers that are  means-tested in various ways there is a  real benefit in that question of de  stigmatizing or to put it another way  it's a question of making people less  reliant upon or subject to the  paternalistic dictates of a welfare  state bureaucracy that makes you jump  through certain hoops whether that's  work requirements or drug testing or  just meeting with caseworkers or filling  out forms to the degree that you know  the unconditional or universal part that  you in you in you be I is important it's  important I think for that kind of  reason now when you look at the range of  proposals for basic income you will see  that there's a wide range of ideas about  exactly what it should or shouldn't  replace so this question of what a basic  income should or shouldn't replace is  pretty important and it bears on the  question of whether a basic income is  really a right wing or a left wing idea  which is something that comes up a lot  in these discussions and that's because  it's one thing to say we should give  everyone you know any amount of basic  income say $1,000 even on top of what  already exists in reality almost every  proposal for a basic income in order to  fund it will say we should replace some  of these cash transfers or near cash  transfers food stamps welfare and so on  but if you get into conservatives like  for example Charles Murray the co-author  of the bell curve in a long time  Washington right wing think-tank person  he wrote a book called in our hands a  decade or more ago in which he proposed  a version of basic income but his  version was called as in the subtitle of  his book a plan to replace the welfare  state by which he meant basically  everything not just food stamps not just  welfare payments but Medicare Medicaid  housing benefits everything would have  to be bought in the market and all you  would get was this face of income  voucher and so that version of basic  income is one that a lot of people on  the Left find disturbing and find to be  a sort of right-wing or neoliberal  Trojan horse to voucherize the entire  welfare state and leave a lot of people  worse off which is why sometimes you get  the distinction that's made that I take  from a collective called the  undercommons that published an article  in Boston review of  this they distinguish between what they  call you bi plus and u bi minus u bi  plus is basically taking the system we  have maybe taking away some of these  conditional cash transfers or  means-tested transfers but leaving in  place you fundamental services like a  right to health care and education and  housing and then supplementing that with  an unconditional cash transfer that  people can use for what they want versus  what they call u bi - which is where  you're essentially taking down the whole  edifice of the welfare state and just  replacing it with a check that will  almost certainly not be enough to cover  it people's needs and also won't be  really a gala terian in the sense that  giving people a voucher to buy  healthcare is unfair in the sense that  different people have different needs  for healthcare depending on whether they  have a chronic illness or whether  they're you know a woman who's going  through a pregnancy or what have you and  so that you may just Roden horse you met  Trojan horse is super interesting to me  because one of the benefits of not means  testing is that you do get to eliminate  bureaucratic waste and all sorts of  infrastructure that is not really  necessary to get people money that could  raise their standard of living or does  raise their standard of living but if  all you're doing is replacing a bunch of  these programs with another one and  eliminating a whole bunch of government  jobs and bureaucracy and waste I like  that idea but it's basically a proxy  towards just small government in a sense  which is a very right-wing idea at its  core well I would say it not necessarily  small government the idea that the size  of government is what matters is I think  what is the right wing idea that like  we're debating whether we're for big  government on the left or small  government on the right Aveda it may not  returns yeah I would argue I'm for small  government in the sense that I want to  drastically cut the military budget  right and I'm also for small government  in the sense that I think there is  something to the libertarian critique  which you can also find in you know left  critics like say Michel Foucault to the  extent that the state to the extent that  the state controls people and sort of  patronizes and paternalistically manages  people's lives in order so that people  can access benefits or access what they  need to live that is  and impediment to human freedom that is  not something we should desire and so  insofar as we can do redistribution with  unconditional transfers that don't  require so much bureaucracy and so much  micromanaging of people's lives I think  that that's a left-wing idea  I don't disagree with you yeah because I  I think we're on the same page as far as  that goes  to talk about the other side of the  equation a little bit what is it that  you see as the most sort of pressing  realization that should be part of the  conversation that isn't about what the  relationship of humans to work will  likely have to be modified to be in the  next 5 10 15 20 30 50 years whatever  timeframe you want to sort of sort of  look at what's one of the fundamental  changes that we are going to have to  come to terms with about how work fits  into our lives that's not