ms like this is  going directly to the core of whether  you believe in markets or not or to  which degree you believe in markets that  are how free in a sense because there is  a huge sort of socio-political  contingent that works under the umbrella  of the market determines what has value  and if something doesn't have  quantifiable value in some kind of  market it doesn't actually have worth  and and doesn't that fundamentally need  to be upended if we are seriously going  to have this discussion and rethink what  exactly what you're talking about I  think we do have to have that discussion  certainly and there's a variety of  reasons why we should be considering the  value of things that are not necessarily  valued on the market such as for example  as I mentioned reproductive labor which  is the labor that produces the workforce  that is the input to the entire rest of  the economy so in that sense it has  value and yet monetary value is not  historically placed on it by the market  for ecologic 'el reasons of course we  have to think about all the ways in  which ecological costs environmental  costs are not priced by the market in a  way that is sustainable but I think when  we're talking about markets I want to  make a clear distinction between the  idea that things are bought and sold  commodities have prices services have  prices that sort of thing because that  kind of thing can exists in many  different forms of social relations many  different kinds of economies the one  market that I am primarily concerned  with and that I think bears on this  question of basic income of the future  of work and jobs is the labor market  right fact that the marketplace is not  just a price  on the things you own or the things you  produce or the things you buy it places  a price on you and that in a society in  which people mostly do not have a way to  support themselves unless they directly  or indirectly can access paying jobs on  the labour market then yes we're  basically saying we're comfortable with  a society in which the value of human  beings is determined by their  marketability and it's that fundamental  feature of the particular capitalist  market in which we exist is that labor  market that I think is most important to  contest we've been speaking with Peter  frays who's an editor jacoba magazine  also author of the book for futures from  verso press Peter really important  conversation I'm glad it's being had  more and I'm glad to be able to continue  talking about it with you as well yeah  in time thanks for having me back

